      "id": "summary",
      "proposition": {
        "predicate": "summary",
        "object": "<p>Assisting an ageing loved one can start with small tasks but may lead to full-time caregiving that requires more support</p><p>Helping an ageing parent or loved one often begins with easy-to-field requests: Pick up a prescription. Drive them to a doctor’s appointment. Grab groceries.</p><p>However, light tasks – just “helping out” here and there – may eventually snowball into the avalanche of full-time caregiving, a phenomenon becoming known as “caregiver creep”. Like “<a href=\"https://www.caregiveraction.org/understanding-caregiver-burnout/\">caregiver burnout</a>”, it’s not a formal term, but there is growing awareness about this slow build among healthcare providers and experts.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/wellness/2026/aug/31/caregiver-creep-caring-elderly-relatives\">Continue reading...</a>"
